  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "My Back Door Isn't Used by the Public – Does It Still Need to Be Accessible?",
    "text": "My Back Door Isn't Used by the Public – Does It Still Need to Be Accessible?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "This is a really common question, and the answer isn't always a simple yes or no! Many people assume that if a back entrance isn't for the general public, it's automatically exempt from accessibility requirements. However, that's not necessarily true. The key often lies in who *does* use it. If employees regularly use that back entrance, or if it's the only accessible route to a specific altered area within your building, then it might indeed need to be made accessible. It really comes down to assessing its function. First, you'll need to figure out if it even counts as an 'entrance' under the relevant accessibility rules for your situation. Then, you'll want to check the entire route leading to and from that door. If you're doing any alteration work in a primary function area, you could even have 'path-of-travel' obligations that might mean making another entrance accessible, even if it's not the one you're working on directly. Don't make the mistake of assuming a 'back door' is automatically exempt, or overlooking employee use. These are frequent missteps that can lead to issues down the line. When you're facing these kinds of questions, especially when considering ramp needs, door hardware, or general code coordination, it's wise to bring in a qualified professional. They can help you understand all the nuances and ensure your project meets all necessary standards.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}